Reasoning

1. No repayment in year 10  
   Any loan that matures after the impact date has a 0 % probability of being repaid.  Lenders therefore will not roll-over or originate new debt whose maturity exceeds the remaining life-span (≈ 10 years).  The market for 10-year and longer paper simply disappears.

2. Short-maturity debt becomes extremely scarce  
   Because everybody knows the long end is worthless, every borrower tries to shift his financing to the very short end (overnight, 1-week, 1-month).  At the same time lenders, facing the same terminal date, refuse to part with liquidity for even a few months unless they are compensated for the fact that the money will almost certainly never come back.  The supply of short-maturity funds collapses and the demand for it explodes.

3. The “time-value of money” flips sign  
   In normal times you accept less money today in exchange for more money tomorrow.  When tomorrow may not exist, the only contractual form that survives is “give me resources right now; I will return them (with whatever interest) only if the earth is still here.”  That is equivalent to a very large, possibly infinite, discount rate on future claims.  The spot price of future money falls toward zero, so the implied interest rate on any loan that is still being rolled over approaches +∞.

4. Collateral and confiscation risk reinforce the spike  
   Even for loans that mature well before year 10, the probability that the borrower (or the lender) will be physically wiped out, or that legal enforcement will break down, is rising rapidly.  The survival probability p(t) enters every lender’s zero-profit condition  
   (1 + r_t) · p(t) = 1 + ρ,  
   where ρ is the lender’s required return on surviving states.  As p(t)→0, r_t→∞.  For t close to 10, p(t) is essentially zero, so the quoted rate is “infinite” in the sense that no finite coupon can clear the market.

5. Central-bank rate cuts would be ineffective  
   A central bank could try to flood the system with reserves, but with no prospect of repayment beyond the horizon nobody is willing to hold even overnight claims on private counterparties.  The policy rate therefore loses its anchor; the inter-bank market freezes at whatever bid is posted by the most desperate borrower.  Historically we have seen overnight rates jump into the hundreds of percent in wartime or hyperinflationary collapses; here the terminal certainty makes those episodes look mild.

6. Government bills might trade at still-positive prices  
   A 3-month Treasury bill could still have value because the government can seize real resources today and repay with newly printed money in three months.  But even that bill must carry a huge default premium, so its yield would still be orders of magnitude above today’s levels.

Conclusions

- Any interest rate quoted for a maturity that approaches ten years becomes effectively infinite; those markets cease to exist.  
- For shorter maturities that still clear, equilibrium rates explode to triple-digit levels or higher, limited only by the legal maximum or by the point at which borrowers simply give up.  
- The entire yield curve does not “shift up” uniformly; it kinks so violently that only the ultra-short segment is still traded, and at astronomical yields. [end of text]


